tness: A Prospective Codec-Stress Evaluation of Audio Attribution\n=========================================================================================================================\n\nAuthors:[Gang Shi](https://arxiv.org/search/cs?searchtype=author&query=Shi,+G) (Independent Researcher)\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled Clean Accuracy Does Not Guarantee Provenance Robustness: A Prospective Codec-Stress Evaluation of Audio Attribution, by Gang Shi (Independent Researcher)\n\n[View PDF](/pdf/2609.07981) [HTML (experimental)](https://arxiv.org/html/2609.07981v1)\n\n> Abstract:Audio provenance attribution - which system produced a synthetic utterance - is reported at near-ceiling accuracy on clean benchmarks, yet audio reaching an analyst has usually been transcoded. We report a prospectively registered measurement of closed-set attribution after single-stage codec transport, with the analysis region fixed from fidelity metadata before any attribution model was trained. On two corpora, in-support losses reach 53.5 \\[43.5, 63.6\\] and 70.3 \\[63.0, 77.5\\] Macro-F1 points for WavLM-Base+, and 61.0 \\[56.8, 65.1\\] and 49.8 \\[41.6, 57.9\\] for W2V2-BERT 2.0, under simultaneous component-level bands. Degradation is strongly condition- and representation-dependent: within one in-support grid WavLM losses run from -0.4 to +53.5 points, and the two encoders differ beyond a prespecified +/-5-point margin at six of twelve conditions. A clean-qualified ECAPA-TDNN and a Proxy-Anchor head degrade comparably, so the effect is not confined to one representation family or a weak linear head. The registered matched-fidelity comparison was not estimable on this grid, and waveform and perceptual measures order the conditions differently: MP3 at 8 kbit/s ranks mid-grid on SI-SDR but last on PESQ-WB while causing the largest loss. For the tested tasks, corpora, representations and codec grid, a clean accuracy figure does not by itself characterise deployment robustness.\n\nComments:\n\n13 pages, 5 figures\n\nSubjects:\n\nSound (cs.SD); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\n\nCite as:\n\n[arXiv:2609.07981](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.07981) \\[cs.SD\\]\n\n \n\n(or [arXiv:2609.07981v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.07981v1) \\[cs.SD\\] for this version)\n\n \n\n[https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2609.07981](https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2609.07981)\n\nFocus to learn more\n\narXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\n\nSubmission history\n------------------\n\nFrom: Gang Shi \\[[view email](/show-email/7204b4f0/2609.07981)\\]  \n**\\[v1\\]** Mon, 7 Sep 2026 21:01:00 UTC (85 KB)  \n\nFull-text links:\n\nAccess Paper:\n-------------\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled Clean Accuracy Does Not Guarantee Provenance Robustness: A Prospective Codec-Stress Evaluation of Audio Attribution, by Gang Shi (Independent Researcher)\n\n*   [View PDF](/pdf/2609.07981)\n*   [HTML (experimental)](https://arxiv.org/html/2609.07981v1)\n*   [TeX Source](/src/2609.07981)\n\n[view license](http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/ \"Rights to this article\")\n\n### Additional Features\n\n*   [Audio Summary](/audio/2609.07981)\n\n### Current browse context:\n\ncs.SD\n\n[< prev](/prevnext?id=2609.07981&function=prev&context=cs.SD \"previous in cs.SD (accesskey p)\")   |   [next >](/prevnext?id=2609.07981&function=next&context=cs.SD \"next in cs.SD (accesskey n)\")  \n\n[new](/list/cs.SD/new) | [recent](/list/cs.SD/recent) | [2026-09](/list/cs.SD/2026-09)\n\nChange to browse by:\n\n[cs](/abs/2609.07981?context=cs)  \n[cs.LG](/abs/2609.07981?context=cs.LG)  \n\n### References & Citations\n\n*   [SciX](https://scixplorer.org/abs/arXiv:2609.07981)\n*   [Google Scholar](https://scholar.google.com/scholar_lookup?arxiv_id=2609.07981)\n*   [Semantic Scholar](https://api.semanticscholar.org/arXiv:2609.07981)\n\nexport BibTeX citation Loading...\n\nBibTeX formatted citation\n-------------------------\n\n×\n\nloading...\n\nData provided by:\n\n### Bookmark\n\n [![BibSonomy](/static/browse/0.3.4/image