{"parentUuid":"10df19b4-082c-4bcc-8879-f52c312b380b","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a5e09ecae0f30c721","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgkZjLhKcU7NMB9gqy7pL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DUwo8RsUibWq7S5Pn1wnbR","name":"SubagentHandback","input":{"message":"Report on three papers. Full PDF text was not readable for any of them. The abstracts and the HTML versions were read, and the HTML versions were only partly verbatim. Quotes are short, and \"[S]\" marks content from the tool's model-generated summary of the unread remainder, not verbatim text.\n\nACCESS\n- A (arXiv 2503.17577v2, CODASPY '26): PDF returned as binary and was saved locally, so no text. Read abstract page https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.17577 and HTML https://arxiv.org/html/2503.17577v2 verbatim to about 57% (39,423 of 69,314 chars). The rest is summarized [S].\n- B (arXiv 2609.07981v1, single author, submitted 7 Sep 2026): PDF saved locally, no text. Read https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.07981 and https://arxiv.org/html/2609.07981v1 verbatim to about 48% (39,423 of 82,354). Rest [S]. Sections XI (Limitations) and XIII (Data and Code) were not read verbatim.\n- C (arXiv 2601.19017v1): PDF at https://arxiv.org/pdf/2601.19017 failed with \"maxContentLength size of 10485760 exceeded\" (permanent, not retried). Read https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.19017 and https://arxiv.org/html/2601.19017v1 verbatim to about 43% (39,423 of 90,596). Rest [S].\n\nA. Li, Chen, Wei, \"Measuring the Robustness of Audio Deepfake Detection under Real-World Corruption\"\n1) Metrics: EER (%), Accuracy at the EER threshold, AUROC on clean and corrupted Wavefake test data. Quality: SNR and ViSQOL. Quoted: \"A ViSQOL score of 3 or above typically represents acceptable audio quality.\" ViSQOL>=3 is used only as a quality gate (corruption levels kept at ViSQOL>=3). No numeric threshold for \"robust\", \"unchanged\" or \"significant\", and no equivalence margin.\n2) Perturbations (Table 1, verbatim): Gaussian/background noise and background music at SNR [5, 40] dB; pitch shift [-2, 2] semitones; time stretch [0.7, 1.5]; echo delay [0.1, 0.9] s; low/high-pass cutoff ratio [0.1, 0.5]; silence insertion ratio [0.1, 0.5]; smoothing window [6, 26]; MP3 bitrate [8, 40] kbps; Opus [16, 256] kbps; quantization 2–10 bits; EnCodec bandwidth [1.5, 24.0] kHz; AudioDec, FACodec, DAC at \"default\" bitrate. Verbatim: \"even when audio is compressed to as low as 8 kbps\"; \"For Opus, even at a high bitrate of 256 kbps, detection performance remains limited.\" No random-perturbation or random-model control is mentioned. Clean reference is Table 2 (EER %): RawNet2 5.839, Wave2Vec2 1.259, Whisper 0.649, AASIST 0.076, Wave2Vec2BERT 0.038. Data: WaveFake, about 196 h, 0.7/0.1/0.2 split, \"we focus on a single dataset to isolate corruption effects.\"\n3) Statistics: none in the verbatim portion. No CIs, bootstrap, significance tests, seeds or pre-registration [S, consistent with the verbatim text read].\n4) Headlines (abstract, four insights): \"Most models are robust to noise but remain vulnerable to audio modifications and compression, especially neural codecs.\" Foundation models generally outperform traditional ones; larger models help with \"diminishing returns\"; augmentation or speech enhancement help. Verbatim examples: echo at 0.1 s gives Wave2Vec2 Accuracy 0.558, HuBERT 0.823, Whisper 0.9. Limitations: one dataset; ElevenLabs commercial-detector test uses 10 samples per perturbation and severity [S]; the summary flags an internal inconsistency (high-pass vs time stretch) and a duplicated figure image [S].\n\nB. G. Shi, \"Clean Accuracy Does Not Guarantee Provenance Robustness: A Prospective Codec-Stress Evaluation of Audio Attribution\"\n1) Metric: Macro-F1 change, Δ = clean − transformed, in points; positive means loss. Macro-F1 is multiclass over retained classes. Thresholds, all fixed before execution: clean gate Macro-F1 0.85; \"the 20-point simultaneous lower bound\" for a material stress loss; ±5-point encoder-interaction margin; δ_D = 5 points for association hypotheses. No separate justification in the excerpt [S]. Interval must exceed the threshold, not the point estimate.\n2) Codecs and rates (Table III verbatim): EnCodec 24, 6, 3, 1.5 kbit/s; DAC 6.1, 2.8, 1.4 kbit/s (nq=13, 6, 3 codebooks) and dac_nq20 at 9.4 kbit/s. MP3 and Opus named by nominal bitrate (e.g., mp3_8 = MP3 at 8 kbit/s; Opus-6, Opus-16 also verbatim). Other MP3/Opus rates appear in tables [S]. Clips 16 kHz mono; MP3/Opus via ffmpeg 7.0.2; neural codecs via 16→24→16 kHz resampling. Controls (verbatim): \"a 4 kHz bandwidth bottleneck, 8-bit µ-law, and additive noise at 10 dB and 5 dB SI-SDR.\" Table IV: lowpass_4k residual +14.8 [+6.2, +23.4] (\"more degradation than the fidelity-only account predicts\"); mulaw_8bit +13.6 [-5.8, +33.0] inconclusive; noise_sisdr10 -3.4 [-12.0, +5.3] inconclusive; noise_sisdr5 -19.5 [-30.6, ...] (cut off). These are residuals from a fidelity-only model M0. Encoders: WavLM-Base+, W2V2-BERT 2.0 (deciding); ECAPA-TDNN and Proxy-Anchor (secondary). Single-stage transforms only; cascades not tested.\n3) Statistics: component-level cluster bootstrap, B = 1000 (verbatim). Clips are not the sample: \"45 independent components on LA and 28 on ST-Codecfake-OOD.\" Simultaneous 95% bands via max-|t| over the registered family. Five seeds yield only \"three unique split configurations on each corpus\" with duplicated weight; equal-weighting sensitivity is reported and \"changes no primary decision.\" No p-values for Q1b [S]. Q2 uses an intersection-union test [S].\nProspective meaning: \"prospectively registered measurement\"; support box \"fixed from fidelity metadata before any attribution model was trained\"; the mask was \"hashed and committed\" before attribution heads were trained. Development and prospective data were kept separate. Registered matched-fidelity comparison used tolerances |ΔSI-SDR| <= 2.0 dB, |ΔPESQ-WB| <= 0.35, |ΔSTOI| <= 0.05, |Δbandwidth| <= 1.0 kHz, and was \"not estimable\": calibration gave one pair per corpus against a gate of two. The Q1b support rule was chosen on calibration data after a draft failed, so it is post-registration and secondary. The overwrite rule (Q2) was frozen as code verified byte-identical. Registration venue/date and protocol hashes were not found in the parts I read; the summary says they are promised for \"a future revision\" [S].\n4) Headlines (abstract): WavLM-Base+ \"53.5 [43.5, 63.6]\" (LA, mp3_8) and \"70.3 [63.0, 77.5]\" (ST, mp3_8); W2V2-BERT 2.0 \"61.0 [56.8, 65.1]\" (LA, Opus-6) and \"49.8 [41.6, 57.9]\" (ST, mp3_8). Clean Macro-F1 >0.85 for all encoders (Table II; 0.856–0.982). Losses range from -0.4 to +53.5 on WavLM (LA). Encoders differ beyond ±5 points at six of twelve LA conditions. Fidelity metrics disagree: MP3 8 kbit/s \"last on PESQ-WB\" but \"mid-grid on SI-SDR.\" Q1b H4 supported but about one point of explained variance; H1–H3 inconclusive; Q2 overwrite not confirmed [S]. Limitations (verbatim/abstract): two corpora, closed-set only, single-stage codecs, small effective N, matched comparison non-estimable, a disclosed Q2 implementation issue on ST under DAC [S]. Author is an independent researcher without institutional support (verbatim).\n\nC. Buck et al., \"A Framework for Evaluating Faithfulness in Explainable AI for Machine Anomalous Sound Detection Using Frequency-Band Perturbation\"\n1) Metrics: Spearman ρs between each band's mean XAI relevance and ΔPred, where ΔPred = |Pred_original − Pred_sample| [S]. Model quality: AUC and pAUC (Dev/Eval). Significance: two-tailed p < 0.05 [S], with no justification and no equivalence threshold for \"unchanged.\" Verbatim: \"The proposed framework offers a reproducible way to benchmark audio explanations.\"\n2) Perturbation: five linear 1600 Hz bands over 0–8000 Hz; set normalised magnitude in the band to zero; the model is retrained on modified spectrograms in Exp. 2 (verbatim: \"excluding successive frequency bands of width 1600 Hz\"). Whether Exp. 3 retrains is unclear [S]. Fill value zero is not justified or compared against alternatives. XAI methods: Integrated Gradients (zero baseline), Occlusion, Grad-CAM, SmoothGrad. Model: Wilkinghoff et al. FeatEx self-supervised model on DCASE 2023 Task 2 (14 machine types). Controls: no random-band or random-attribution baseline reported [S]. The Occlusion window size is not given in the excerpt.\n3) Statistics: no CIs, bootstrap, significance correction across 16 machines × 4 methods, pre-registration or seed variance reported [S]. Verbatim: \"Two-tailed\" is not quoted but the summary flags p-values inconsistent with n=200 (ToyNscale Grad-CAM ρ=-0.154 with p=0.000; Shaker IG ρ=0.449 with p=0.770). Those are from the summary and should be verified against Table 4.\n4) Headlines: abstract says \"Occlusion demonstrating the strongest alignment with true model sensitivity and gradient-based methods often failing.\" Summary numbers [S]: Occlusion overall ρ̄s = 0.884; Grad-CAM 0.536 (negative on ToyNscale −0.154 and ToyTrain −0.253); Integrated Gradients 0.530; SmoothGrad 0.400. Exp. 2: full-spectrum Dev AUC 73.5%; removing band 1 gives 63.0%. Limitations: the authors say Occlusion is \"partly expected\" to score highly because it mirrors the perturbation ground truth, so the benchmark is partly circular. Only frequency-band masks, linear bands, one architecture and one dataset; the authors claim \"the first systematic evaluation\" of XAI faithfulness for machine ASD.\n\nSummary of gaps: none of the three papers, as read, gives a CI or bootstrap on the headline change metric except B. A and C report no interval or significance test in the parts I read. A and C give no pre-registration. B's \"prospective\" claim is about registered thresholds and split discipline, not an external registry in the parts I read."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","s